{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And they said: We have sinned, for we have spoken against HaShem and against you.</b> They sought forgiveness from Moses and confessed their sin, and Moses prayed for them, as it is said, ״ויתפלל משה בעד העם״. From this we learn that one from whom forgiveness is sought should not be cruel. And so we find (Genesis 20) that Abraham prayed for Abimelech, and likewise Job (42) when he prayed on behalf of his friend; and if one does not forgive he is called a sinner, as it is said (1 Samuel 12): ״גם אנכי חלילה לי מחטוא לה׳ מחדול להתפלל בעדכם״. And Scripture there mentions in the matter of Samuel that Israel said to him, ״we have sinned.״" "<b>And remove from us the serpent.</b> He should have said 'the serpents,' but he mentioned it in the singular because it alludes to the serpent on high — it is the power of death, the prince of the wilderness, where the dwelling-place of the fiery serpents is. And this is the force of ״מעלינו״ — and he did not say ויסיר ממנו, in the manner of (Numbers 14) ״סר צלם מעליהם״. And from this you will understand (Zechariah 6) ״וההרים הרי נחשת״, which the Guide of the Perplexed mentions in chapter ten — it is the great mountain that is destined to become a plain before Zerubbabel."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:33.352262701Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}